cellPad: the fps layout reads its keys through a table of bindings the host can replace (pad_keymap.h).

The fps layout's keys, mouse buttons and wheel were a fixed map in pad_keyboard_fps, and a player of Resistance 3 who
wants crouch on another key, fire on a side button or the movement keys somewhere else has no way to say so. Now the
layout is a table of bindings: a source (a key, a mouse button, one way of the wheel) that presses an action (a pad
button, a direction of the left stick, or CONFIRM/BACK, which are CROSS/CIRCLE while a menu is up and START otherwise
and keep what they got until the key is released). The built-in table is the layout as it was;
cellPad_default_key_bindings copies it out for a host to start from and cellPad_set_key_bindings replaces it. The
host's own key names and file are its business (Resistance 3: host/r3_controls.cpp). The mouse's aim scale
(PAD_MOUSE_AIM_SCALE) follows L1 instead of the right mouse button, so it still means "while aiming" when the aim
button is bound elsewhere.

Test: libs/input/tests/test_pad_keyboard_fps.c test_bindings (the table copied out, a replacement, entries without a
source or an action skipped, the cap, CONFIRM/BACK on other keys, a wheel direction of its own, aim on a key); the
older checks pass unchanged, as do the other pad tests and tools/test_pad_file_reports.py. Only the macOS key codes are
exercised here; the table holds each host's own key codes as before.

Upstream: not proposed yet.
